  "style": "high Renaissance",
  "material": "oil on wood",
  "dimensions": "56.8 × 55 cm",
  "production_date": "about 1500",
  "brief_description": "This scene is so unusual that it's not entirely clear who the figures are intended to represent. Usually the Three Kings are shown kneeling in front of the Christ Child; here, instead, we can see two of them standing proudly before the Virgin and Child, presenting their gifts in large containers. We aren't sure which figure is intended to represent the third king.\n\nThe figure with the mane of hair who looks straight towards us is John the Baptist. It's unusual to see him in this scene, but his baptism of Christ is celebrated on 6 January -- the same day that the visit of the Three Kings is celebrated.\n\nBramantino was fascinated by linear perspective (using line to create an impression of three-dimensional space on a flat surface). He created a guide of intersecting lines on this panel, allowing him to paint objects and figures at the correct scale relative to each other so they appear to be in a realistic three-dimensional space.",
  "accession_date": "1916-01-01",
  "current_location": "Room 14, The National Gallery (London)",
  "credit_line": "Layard Bequest, 1916",
  "associated_concepts": "Italian (Lombard)",
